    "---\n",
    "## 6. 🧩 Decorators <a id='6'></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "### What & Why\n",
    "A decorator is a function that **wraps another function**, adding behavior before and/or after it runs — without modifying the original function's source code.\n",
    "\n",
    "Syntactically, `@my_decorator` above a function is shorthand for `fn = my_decorator(fn)`.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why Decorators Are Everywhere in Data Engineering\n",
    "- **Retry logic** — wrap any DB/API call with automatic retries\n",
    "- **Logging** — log every function call, its args, and return value\n",
    "- **Timing** — instrument any function for performance monitoring\n",
    "- **Validation** — enforce data contracts at function boundaries\n",
    "- **Authentication** — add auth checks to API endpoint handlers\n",
    "- **Rate limiting** — throttle API calls automatically\n",
    "\n",
    "### The key rule\n",
    "Always use `@functools.wraps(fn)` inside your decorator wrapper. Without it, the wrapped function loses its `__name__`, `__doc__`, and other metadata — which breaks logging and debugging."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "concept6-code1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import functools, time, logging\n",
    "\n",
    "logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format='%(levelname)s: %(message)s')\n",
    "log = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── Simple decorator: execution timer ────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "def timed(fn):\n",
    "    @functools.wraps(fn)   # <-- Preserves fn.__name__, fn.__doc__, etc.\n",
    "    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n",
    "        start = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "        result = fn(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "        elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n",
    "        log.info(f\"{fn.__name__} completed in {elapsed:.4f}s\")\n",
    "        return result\n",
    "    return wrapper\n",
    "\n",
    "@timed\n",
    "def load_data(source: str) -> list:\n",
    "    time.sleep(0.05)  # Simulated I/O\n",
    "    return list(range(1000))\n",
    "\n",
    "data = load_data(\"s3://my-bucket/file.parquet\")\n",
    "print(f\"Loaded {len(data)} records\")"
   ]
