    "---\n",
    "## 7. 🗂️ File I/O & Serialization <a id='7'></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "### What & Why\n",
    "Data engineers spend a significant portion of their time reading from and writing to files. Knowing the right format and library for each scenario is critical for performance and interoperability.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Format | Best for | Pros | Cons |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **CSV** | Simple tabular data, human-readable | Universal support | No types, slow at scale |\n",
    "| **JSON** | API responses, configs, nested data | Flexible, human-readable | Verbose, slow for large data |\n",
    "| **Parquet** | Analytical workloads, data lakes | Columnar, compressed, typed | Not human-readable |\n",
    "| **Avro** | Streaming (Kafka), schema evolution | Compact, schema embedded | Requires schema registry |\n",
    "| **JSONL** | Log files, streaming records | Line-by-line processing | Larger than Parquet |\n",
    "| **Pickle** | Python objects (ML models, caches) | Any Python object | Python-only, security risk |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key principle: Chunked reading\n",
    "Never load a file fully into memory if you don't need to. Always use chunked or streaming reads for large files."
   ]
  },
